# CI Commit Prefix Convention

When creating git commits for changes to workflow or action YAML files, use the `fix(ci):` prefix. This ensures dependency updates and CI configuration changes trigger a patch release via conventional-changelog (the `chore:` and `ci:` prefixes do not produce a version bump).

## Examples

```
fix(ci): update actions/setup-node to v6
fix(ci): disable package manager cache in e2e workflow
fix(ci): pin third-party action dependencies
```

## Why not `chore(ci):`?

The release workflow uses `TriPSs/conventional-changelog-action` with the `conventionalcommits` preset. Only `feat:`, `fix:`, and `perf:` prefixes trigger version bumps. Using `chore:` or `ci:` means the change is invisible to the release process and consumers never get a new tag to pin to.
